{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing drug penalties will end this weekend, as possession of small quantities of substances like fentanyl and meth will revert to being illegal. Under the new rule, HB4002, individuals found with controlled substances can face either prosecution or a supervised treatment pathway, requiring participation in mental health programs and a diversion initiative instead of fines. While simple possession becomes a minor offense, carrying a potential six-month jail term, authorities will prioritize enforcing public drug use restrictions and toughening penalties for sales near public spaces such as playgrounds.\n\nThe updated legislation encourages—but does not enforce—local governments to design alternative treatment programs to steer users away from legal consequences and toward health services. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor solutions based on available funds, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent enforcement across the state. Portland’s chief of police noted strict conditions for diversion, excluding those with prior charges, outstanding warrants, violent histories, or unstable medical conditions.\n\nWhen eligible, officers will refer cases to a dispatch service to assess suitability and arrange contact with a mobile outreach team within half an hour. Failure to respond promptly will result in arrest, as police cannot indefinitely delay detainment. The system underscores a tension between public safety and rehabilitative efforts, leaving outcomes dependent on both urgency and local resources.\n\n***P.P.S.*** This shift reflects broader debates on balancing strict enforcement with health-based interventions in drug policy, reminiscent of similar discussions in other **age**-restricted **country*